00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.150
Thank you all for
coming here today.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.840
I wanted this to be a little
bit of a different session

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.990
than other sessions.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.350
A lot of sessions will
go deep on one product

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.200
or go deep on
something technically.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.470
This session is really
meant to piece everything

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.620
that you've been seeing together
over the last year, year

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.302
and a half, the
announcements we had today,

00:00:40.302 --> 00:00:41.760
and where we're
going in the future

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.760
and give you a little bit of
the thought process behind all

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.450
of this, the data that we
use, and just a little peek

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:49.980
behind the curtain.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.020
First though, I'm going
to go a little bit meta.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.690
I am going to for the
next 30-ish minutes

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.800
try and get whatever is in my
head into all of your heads.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.370
That's what a
presentation about, right?

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.370
The speaker is trying to
get the content that he

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.290
has into the audience's mind.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.210
This notion of trying
to get what's in my head

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.860
into all of your
heads, it's part

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.260
of something we like to
call capturing and conveying

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.640
thought.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.370
And really, it's what the
entire productivity market

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.710
is based on, trying
to get you to be

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.560
able to capture and convey
thought more effectively.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.701
But yet, there's this
irony of the situation

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:33.450
because this is
productivity market, which

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.980
is trying to become
more efficient,

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:40.020
but there's no real
metric, no real number

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.330
that says, hey, you're
doing this more efficiently.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.680
Actually by switching
to these tools,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.810
you're actually making your
company more efficient.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.170
We don't actually have that.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.220
There are tools that
say, OK, this is

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.710
how you're spending your time.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.430
In fact, there's also
negative metrics, right?

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.930
So I don't know if
you've heard this

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.320
before but a notification
can break you from your flow.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.840
And it may take you 20,
30, or even 40 minutes

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.114
to get back into the state of
high throughput production.

00:02:10.114 --> 00:02:12.030
Now, that's interesting,
but it doesn't really

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.560
help you justify an ROI
on productivity software

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.400
or a switch to certain
productivity software.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.990
Now, this is where Google
has a unique vantage point.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.610
We have some genuine
insights here

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.400
because we spend a lot of
time and resources on this.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.670
How many of you are familiar
with the "New York Times"

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.279
study on Project Aristotle?

00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:34.820
OK, well, the people
in the front row

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.360
are going to have to help
me inform everyone else.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.210
I will give you a
high level here.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.780
Google did a study starting
in 2012 where we actually

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.880
studied how our teams work.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.470
And being a data
driven company, we

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.740
obsessed and analyzed
everything that our teams did.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.120
And we learned a ton.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.620
And we realized
that we actually had

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.630
to take a lot of these
processes to the enterprise.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.260
And it was the justification for
us starting the Early Adopter

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.844
Program, which some of you are
probably familiar at this point

00:02:59.844 --> 00:03:01.884
because we launch a lot
of our products that way.

00:03:01.884 --> 00:03:03.080
It started about a year ago.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.371
And the notion behind this
is that if we have use cases

00:03:05.371 --> 00:03:07.340
that we're trying to
solve, how do we make sure

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.923
that we get them in
front of customers

00:03:08.923 --> 00:03:11.500
and get the feedback in
order to actually craft

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.591
the future solutions?

00:03:13.591 --> 00:03:15.590
And that's great from a
qualitative perspective.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.480
But again, we're a
data driven company.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.870
We also have the benefit of
being a Cloud-native suite

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.810
meaning that when we deploy
a solution to all of you,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.630
we actually can see, hey, did
the behavior changes that we

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:27.830
intended actually happen.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.110
We can actually look
at usage patterns

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:33.810
and look at how our products
are actually being used.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.400
Now, something
interesting came out

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.160
when we actually looked at
this usage data, another tidbit

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.950
that we didn't quite expect.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.750
And that is that
most people spend

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.779
their time not on the actual
ideas that you have, right?

00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:47.820
We all have unique ideas.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.200
That is our value.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.480
But they don't spend most
of their time on that.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.090
They spend most of their time
learning and manipulating

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.510
tools for rather mundane tasks.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:01.701
Let's walk through
an example of this.

00:04:01.701 --> 00:04:03.450
Scheduling a meeting,
I'm sure none of you

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.230
are familiar with
this process, right?

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.396
Never had to schedule a
meeting before in your life.

00:04:07.396 --> 00:04:09.900
So I'm going to teach you about
how you schedule a meeting.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.980
I got some smiles there.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.550
Yes, this is a process we all
know and it's really painful.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:15.180
What happens?

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.638
You ever try and
schedule a meeting

00:04:16.638 --> 00:04:19.560
with I don't know 5, 10 people
across different sites, maybe

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.990
different time
zones, try and find

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.030
an available time that
works for everyone,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.980
try and find a meeting room.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.180
Good luck with that.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.890
Then try and find the
documents that you

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.020
need for that discussion.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.680
Maybe, it's the meeting
notes from last time.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.180
It's the presentation
that you have.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.270
This can take several
hours in some instances.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.640
And then what happens when
the key people in the meeting

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.150
cancel?

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.230
You just got to go
through that again.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.230
Now, this is just one
example of a mundane task.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.707
There's tons in
our everyday life

00:04:44.707 --> 00:04:46.290
that we probably
just don't even think

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:50.160
about, email management,
formatting documents, finding

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.840
files, expense reports.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.880
It's not hard to
think about places

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.190
where it's not that you're
producing unique ideas.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:00.200
You're just doing the same
tests on a different data set.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:00.700
All right.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.650
So let's make this real.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.335
Going back to data again.

00:05:05.335 --> 00:05:07.210
This is going to be an
audience participation

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.220
point so just yell out here.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.670
When someone creates a
document, what percent of time

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.950
do you think most
people spend actually

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.530
capturing the idea
they have relative

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.483
to other processes
in that document?

00:05:19.483 --> 00:05:20.450
AUDIENCE: 3%.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:22.100
RYAN TABONE: 3%.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.069
16?

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:24.860
I'm also part deaf.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.480
So you're going to
have to project.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:27.110
What's that?

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:27.890
AUDIENCE: 35?

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:29.980
RYAN TABONE: 35%.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.780
Are we doing Price
is Right rules?

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:33.280
AUDIENCE: 0.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:33.905
RYAN TABONE: 0.

00:05:33.905 --> 00:05:34.770
AUDIENCE: $1.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.620
RYAN TABONE: $1.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.370
Yeah, I think we went
full Price is Right.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.590
That wasn't what was
supposed to happen.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:46.610
Well, I think 3% won because
it's actually closer to 5%.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:47.730
The rest of it--

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.231
so 5% spent on your
actual ideation,

00:05:49.231 --> 00:05:51.480
the thing that's valuable,
the whole thing that you're

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.760
trying to convey.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.790
And the rest is spent on various
forms of research, formatting,

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.700
refinement.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.040
And this is not just
unique to documents.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.750
I'm sure that as I'm
talking about this,

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:03.390
you're starting to do
this analysis of yourself

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.930
where you're like
how much time am I

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.960
spending on my actual thought
versus just using tools?

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.190
It happens all the time.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.820
The time that-- Oh,
should I go back?

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.730
I just saw a lot
of phones go down.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:17.336
Yes?

00:06:17.336 --> 00:06:17.960
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.460
RYAN TABONE: All right.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.520
I'm going to give you
five seconds because we're

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:23.560
on a clock here people.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:24.226
No, I'm kidding.

00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:27.510
It's fine.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.010
So this time, this
time that people

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.426
spend on learning
and manipulating

00:06:30.426 --> 00:06:33.030
tools for mundane
tasks, we call it

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:36.390
overhead because of
the obvious opportunity

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:40.290
loss or opportunity cost.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.640
That's the word.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.060
But what if we did a better job
of building tools, ones that

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.374
were cognizant of what
you needed when and maybe

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:50.040
were even cognizant
of who you work with

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.710
and your larger
organizational needs?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.841
Well, then maybe, just maybe,
we could kill overhead.

00:06:56.841 --> 00:06:57.340
All right.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:59.162
Well, how do we do that?

00:06:59.162 --> 00:07:00.870
This is where we turn
to machine learning

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.055
and artificial
intelligence, or ML and AI.

00:07:04.055 --> 00:07:05.680
And I'm sure this is
a surprise, right?

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.450
This is the first time you're
hearing about these terms?

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:09.080
No?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.390
Oh, you didn't hear
it all this morning?

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:11.890
How many of you
skipped the keynote?

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.190
You've heard a lot
about it already.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:19.130
And you'll hear a lot about
it more within the industry.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:21.445
Now, machine learning
for those of you who

00:07:21.445 --> 00:07:23.070
are not familiar with
it at this point,

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:24.153
it may sound intimidating.

00:07:24.153 --> 00:07:27.200
And the math is pretty
involved, but the concept's

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.600
pretty straightforward.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.530
Machine learning algorithms
observe examples.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.340
And then they make predictions.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.050
Simple enough, right?

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.690
Let me give you an example
of how this has worked.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:48.147
So Gmail, a long time ago in
a far, far away place, Gmail

00:07:48.147 --> 00:07:50.230
started studying spam using
these machine learning

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:51.190
algorithms.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.440
And they fed it
this algorithm spam

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.140
emails and non-spam emails.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.569
And guess what
that algorithm did?

00:07:58.569 --> 00:08:00.110
When an incoming
email would come in,

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.050
it could predict whether it
was a spam email or a non-spam.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.884
Layer on many years, and
now we get to a point

00:08:05.884 --> 00:08:07.550
where, hopefully, if
you're using Gmail,

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.200
I'm sure everyone is in
some form in this room,

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.760
you rarely, if ever, see spam.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.320
But we're getting
to a point where

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.153
machine learning and
artificial intelligence

00:08:18.153 --> 00:08:21.150
are making a lot more progress.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.520
I'm sure you've seen
this in the news

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.660
that we are very proud
as a company of having

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.160
market leading machine learning
and artificial intelligence.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.700
There's a lot of press about it.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.990
You probably saw how we were
able to defeat the go champion.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.110
Why does that matter?

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.669
It matters because it shows
that we're now making progress

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.419
fast enough at a point
that we didn't even

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.960
expect that we could start using
machine learning to actually

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.480
make an improvement
directly in our products

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.884
and help you and assist
you in your experience

00:08:48.884 --> 00:08:50.800
as you're using it, not
just in the background

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.560
like in the case of spam.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.570
And we're in a position
to actually use this more

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.640
than others in the
industry because we

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.140
are a Cloud-native platform.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.682
So we have these tools
sitting on these data centers.

00:09:01.682 --> 00:09:03.890
We have these products
sitting on these data centers.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.100
We can actually integrate
them more rapidly,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.870
make big bets because we don't
have any legacy in governments.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.360
And then can again use
that data, the data

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.040
that I mentioned before to see
how we iterate on that cycle.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.248
Now, and the proof as I
mentioned is in our products.

00:09:16.248 --> 00:09:17.800
It's not just that
machine learning

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:21.870
has improved the magic of
Google Search, which it has.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:25.850
It also transformed Google
Translate, transformed

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:30.515
translate, changed Google
Translate overnight.

00:09:30.515 --> 00:09:33.140
Now, of course, there was a lot
of work that led to that point.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.270
But all of the sudden,
overnight people

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.140
couldn't tell the difference
between what Translate had done

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:40.970
and what a human
translator had done.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.830
That's pretty incredible.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:49.001
And we're building this into
our enterprise flows as well.

00:09:49.001 --> 00:09:50.750
Now, when we think
about how to build this

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.610
into our enterprise
flows, we have

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.251
to look at what flows exist.

00:09:55.251 --> 00:09:56.750
And it turns out
that there's really

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:00.860
two types of flows, the
individual and the team.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:02.810
And if you don't
solve for both, you

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.645
don't actually improve
efficiency in the enterprise.

00:10:05.645 --> 00:10:06.770
You need to solve for both.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:10.190
And they're related, but
they're very different problems.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:12.020
Let's first look
at the individual

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.000
focused flows and see
some of the improvements

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:14.708
we've made there.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.240
Smart reply, this feature
was launched in inbox.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.820
And the idea was,
well, let's see

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.430
how people respond to a bunch
of emails again that observe

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.030
examples, make predictions.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.630
And then we could start
making predictions

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.440
about incoming emails,
what you're likely to say.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:35.850
And what's
interesting about this

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.290
again, because we want
to measure and see

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.748
how people respond to it, for
our power users, the ones who

00:10:39.748 --> 00:10:41.550
use this day in,
day out, every day,

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:44.749
they have the ability to save up
to 14 minutes a day in typing.

00:10:44.749 --> 00:10:45.540
That's crazy to me.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.750
I still try and wrap
my head around that.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.950
And Slides Explore.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.380
How many of you have ever
had to make a presentation?

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:58.455
I envy the people who did
not raise their hands.

00:10:58.455 --> 00:11:00.040
And how much time
did you spend just

