This is you, right?

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:48.700
This is just-- She's
like, no, I can't do this

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:50.140
without a whole team of people.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.580
Everyone I've ever
talked to says

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.830
they can't be successful
without working with a team.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:58.120
And these teams are a mix of
wonderful, hopefully wonderful,

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:00.312
people with unique
traits and skills.

00:22:00.312 --> 00:22:02.770
And that's why you're paired
together with them because you

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:05.350
each have different skills
that you can work together

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:07.710
to accomplish your task.

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:09.460
But what does that
actually look like when

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:11.584
you're working with these
other folks in this team?

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:15.830
Does that look somewhat right?

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:17.830
I'm sure most of you
probably haven't mapped out

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:21.260
how your teams work
together, but probably this

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:23.330
resonates a little bit with you.

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:25.670
Meetings, action
items, more meetings,

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:28.280
balancing other projects,
collaborating, scheduling out--

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.160
oh I have to make sure that
I get this done before I

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.450
get something else done.

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:36.530
It's not straightforward.

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:39.431
Now, you see why the team
problem is a different problem.

00:22:39.431 --> 00:22:41.930
Now, let me add overhead to
each one of these pieces, right?

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:43.970
Before, we talked about
meetings and adding overhead

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:45.261
of just scheduling one meeting.

00:22:45.261 --> 00:22:47.270
How about the meeting,
the milestone meeting,

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:50.030
the sub meetings in between,
and then within that everyone's

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:51.320
going to have their
different sets of tools

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:52.820
to do their different
tracks as well

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:54.530
as be able to
prioritize their time

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:55.790
within those different tracks.

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:57.170
And also I, need to
track the action items

00:22:57.170 --> 00:22:58.461
between these different things.

00:22:58.461 --> 00:23:01.280
And, oh, where did I store
those assets because so and so--

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:02.900
it gets to be a mess.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:07.550
In fact, it gets so bad teams
break because of this, right?

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:11.582
I'm waiting on someone
to get my job done.

00:23:11.582 --> 00:23:13.540
They haven't prioritized
the thing accordingly.

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:14.590
We haven't had a
discussion about it

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:16.660
because they can't find
time in their calendar.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.285
I'm going to move on
to something else.

00:23:20.064 --> 00:23:20.980
And then what happens?

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:22.450
Well, now a project that
could've taken a day

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:24.324
is going to take two
weeks because I'm sorry,

00:23:24.324 --> 00:23:25.740
I'm working on something else.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:27.000
I see some heads nodding.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:30.520
I'm sure none of you can
relate to this at all.

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:32.690
All right.

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:36.050
Well, the goal here is to not
only remove that overhead,

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:37.850
but just to really
understand how people

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:41.340
work together, to really
understand their priorities,

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:43.680
to start laying out
their future for them,

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:45.930
to make all of
those steps simpler,

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.480
and fit in with each
other so that you work

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.430
towards a common
goal, and that we

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:53.970
help you achieve that common
goal on a common timeline.

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:58.290
This allows you to focus on
your task as a team, as a unit,

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:01.860
as opposed to the overhead of
the process of each individual

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:03.444
within the unit.

00:24:03.444 --> 00:24:05.110
And when you do that,
it makes an impact

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:07.090
not just on you
as an individual,

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:12.370
but on your entire team and
your entire organization.

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:14.867
And this is why we
relaunched Google Cloud and G

00:24:14.867 --> 00:24:16.730
Suite a few months ago.

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:20.380
It's because we're focusing on
how enterprises work together

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:22.160
in teams.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.331
Now, this may seem
like, oh, well,

00:24:24.331 --> 00:24:26.830
haven't we been solving this
problem for a really long time?

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:27.330
No.

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:29.920
It's actually a relatively
new concept for the market.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:32.230
And, yeah, there are a
few point solutions here,

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:35.617
but really to solve this, you
need a suite working together.

00:24:35.617 --> 00:24:37.450
Because as you see,
there are many different

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:39.658
flows where everyone's using
different sets of tools.

00:24:39.658 --> 00:24:42.190
If they aren't all cognizant
of the common goal,

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:43.940
you're still going to
have those overhead.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.340
And you're still going
to break the process.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.857
So what are we doing
within G Suite?

00:24:48.857 --> 00:24:50.440
Well, it started
with the docs editors

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:52.150
and real-time collaboration.

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:55.570
That may have shifted the entire
industry towards that model

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.630
because they realize it helped
teams work closer together.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.790
But we wanted to take
it to the next level.

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:02.590
And we made a few
announcements today.

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:04.298
For those of you who
were at the keynote,

00:25:04.298 --> 00:25:06.090
consider this somewhat
of a refresher.

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:09.100
Jamboard, now this
is the ability

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:14.229
to actually look at a team space
and combine virtual with real.

00:25:14.229 --> 00:25:16.270
I can be in one room, you
can be in another room,

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:19.284
and what generally happens is
instead of working together

00:25:19.284 --> 00:25:20.950
on a white board, the
people in one room

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.570
work together on a white board
and then we go, trust us,

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:23.134
it's working.

00:25:23.134 --> 00:25:24.300
Trust us, we got this going.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.258
I'm going to take a
picture and send it to you.

00:25:26.258 --> 00:25:27.660
Sure, that's going to happen.

00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:29.465
Or, they write do not
erase on it, right?

00:25:29.465 --> 00:25:31.590
So that when the person
does-- is in the same room,

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.600
they can see it because
that always works.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:39.730
But instead, now, I can
actually join two virtual rooms

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:42.610
into one real room where we
can all work together and then

00:25:42.610 --> 00:25:44.270
resume this work later on.

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:45.760
And we all get copies of it.

00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:50.030
Again, rethinking how
teams work together.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:53.980
But we also went a step further
to lay foundational ground.

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:55.810
Now, this is actually
pretty important

00:25:55.810 --> 00:26:00.880
because this sets a journey
for us that goes on a ways.

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:04.730
When teams work together they
really need two core pieces.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.860
One is a source of
truth and another

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.740
is a way to communicate
about that source of truth

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.600
in order to work towards a goal.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:15.550
Make sense?

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:16.540
Straightforward.

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.164
This is actually like the
complete background for a lot

00:26:19.164 --> 00:26:20.080
of our launches today.

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:23.570
It was that thought process.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:25.550
OK, so let's go
to the first one.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:28.740
Source of truth, team drives.

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:33.060
The idea behind this is I don't
want to think about storage.

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:33.972
I have a team.

00:26:33.972 --> 00:26:35.430
I want to know
where my assets are.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:36.240
Who has ownership?

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:37.140
Wait, do you see that file?

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:37.860
I don't see that file.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:38.443
Is that there?

00:26:38.443 --> 00:26:39.630
No, all of that gone.

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.340
I don't want to worry
about permissions.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.140
It should just work.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:45.570
I should be able to say
I'm going to Team Foo

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.450
and all of my stuff
for Team Foo is there.

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:51.000
In addition, because
we want to make

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:52.932
teams as efficient
as individuals,

00:26:52.932 --> 00:26:54.390
again, we're bringing
quick access,

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.460
which is that thing I
showed you before that has

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:59.510
50% savings to teams as well.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:02.306
OK, now you have
your source of truth,

00:27:02.306 --> 00:27:04.430
you want to communicate
about your source of truth.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:06.540
When we actually
talked to customers,

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.480
we realized-- and to
teams, we realized

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.610
that there's two forms
of communication that

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:13.009
happen on a source of truth.

00:27:13.009 --> 00:27:14.800
For those of you who
saw the announcements,

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:16.799
you know what's coming,
but here's the rationale

00:27:16.799 --> 00:27:18.450
of how we got there.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:22.170
One is a continuous,
asynchronous form

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:25.920
of communication where constant
progress can be made, right?

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:27.750
Let's say I go to a meeting.

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:31.020
And Bob and Sue on my team
still are discussing the problem

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:32.820
at hand, pulling
relevant content

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:33.990
from that source of truth.

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:35.850
They need to be able
to have that discussion

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:36.683
and move it forward.

00:27:36.683 --> 00:27:40.804
Now, if that happens in
one-off conversations,

00:27:40.804 --> 00:27:41.970
I'm not going actually know.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:43.710
And I'm going to hope
someone tells me.

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.810
You actually need a
way to capture what's

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:49.140
going on and being able to
reference back those materials

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:50.942
as well as the
decisions being made.

00:27:50.942 --> 00:27:53.150
And that was the reason why
we launched Hangouts Chat

00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:53.970
together.

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.190
This is a way to make
teams work together

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:59.070
and have one place where
they can coexist and make

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:02.900
constant decisions
and discussions.

00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:05.470
OK, so now you have-- and
also be able to easily locate

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:06.030
this content.

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.820
So you see at the top
a really quick way

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:11.760
to be able to search
and find that content.

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:15.110
The second form of
communication is discreet

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:16.917
face-to-face high bandwidth.

00:28:16.917 --> 00:28:18.500
I need to get five
people in the room.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:19.757
Text just isn't cutting it.

00:28:19.757 --> 00:28:20.840
We got somebody in Taiwan.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:21.930
We got somebody in Europe.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.940
I need to get them
altogether right now.

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:27.002
And it has to be simple,
again, because we want

00:28:27.002 --> 00:28:28.210
to go and kill that overhead.

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:29.876
Because if it takes
10 minutes to set up

00:28:29.876 --> 00:28:35.320
a meeting for 5 people, you just
wasted 50 minutes not just 10

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:36.610
for yourself.

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:39.560
And that's why we
launched Hangouts Meet.

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:42.480
And the idea is two
clicks and you're in,

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:45.420
dial ins for those who
can't make it on the VC,

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:47.550
and best in class
video and audio.

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:51.610
All right.

00:28:51.610 --> 00:28:55.180
Now, that we have those pieces,
we're going to build on them.

00:28:55.180 --> 00:28:57.220
We're going to layer in
things like workflow,

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:59.230
being able to predict
what you need when,

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.840
routing content,
prioritizing and tracking

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.150
tasks, automatically
scheduling blocks of time.

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:06.490
For those of you who saw
the demo today with At Meet,

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:07.906
that exactly what
happened, right?

00:29:07.906 --> 00:29:10.090
That idea of calendaring
now just went away.

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:13.570
I was able to take
five people in a room

00:29:13.570 --> 00:29:15.940
and just create an
instant meeting for us.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:18.910
We can kill this overhead
through these tools.

00:29:18.910 --> 00:29:22.480
And this is the vision to
take world class intelligence

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.840
and bake it into a suite
of tools that work together

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.700
because that's the
only way that we can

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:30.400
get teams to actually
work efficiently

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:33.690
on their different tasks.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:34.380
All right.

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:36.250
Quick wrap up.

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:38.560
Today, we showed
productivity tools actually

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.300
cost more time in
overhead than actually

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:41.940
helping you complete a task.

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:43.690
We can address those
today for individuals

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:44.855
using machine learning.

00:29:44.855 --> 00:29:47.480
And for teams, combining machine
learning with a suite of tools

00:29:47.480 --> 00:29:49.021
is the only way that
we will actually

00:29:49.021 --> 00:29:50.440
be able to address their needs.

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:54.190
And we laid the foundational
pieces with team drives, chat,

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:55.109
and meet.

00:29:55.109 --> 00:29:56.650
Also, interpretive
dance doesn't work

00:29:56.650 --> 00:29:58.620
for telling a presentation.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.370
But this is the start
of a longer journey.

00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:02.890
And I encourage you that part
of the reason why I'm here

00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:05.260
is hopefully you see
where we're headed.

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:07.960
And it makes you want to take
your teams onto these products

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:11.050
now so that as we iterate and
build on top of this stack,

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.870
your team gets the benefit
without having to teach them

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:16.390
something new because we'll
be doing the work for them.

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:20.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

